  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 AM EDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nNo major changes needed to a tranquil and mild forecast. Upper\nlows will hover off the east coast, and develop off of and then\nmove onto the west coast. Those are the anchors of an omega\nblock in the central Conus.\n\nAn active storm track remains across central Canada. Some \nmodels, in the process of diving energy into/reinforcing \ntroffing over far eastern Canada late this week, do bring a \nshortwave across the northern lakes midweek (Wed afternoon/ \nevening). This system doesn't have much moisture to work with, \nand the going forecast will remain dry. But there is some \nguidance (definitely a minority) that squeezes out a smidge of \nQPF over eastern upper MI Wednesday afternoon. So something to \nat least keep an eye on.\n\nOtherwise quiet, warm, and relatively sunny. As surface high \npressure builds east today from ND toward Lk Superior, the \npressure gradient relaxes considerably. That process has already\ngotten underway, and surface winds today will be considerably\nlighter than Sunday. A bit more cloud cover possible on \nWednesday, especially in the north. Max temps on Sunday \noverachieved a bit, not unusual in full sun. Have boosted \nguidance temps a bit over the next couple of days a degree or \ntwo.\n\nNext chance for rain, and it isn't much at this point, is out\ntoward Sat night/Sunday, again mainly in the eastern UP.",
